\section{Export and share}
\label{tut:export-and-share}

Sharing a log comes in two main forms: as a portable file you hand to
someone, or as a remote repository that multiple machines can push to
and pull from.
This tutorial keeps the project constant and varies only the delivery
mechanism so the learner can discern when each workflow fits.
The critical aspect is the sharing method: the tutorial first contrasts
bundle export with ProgSnap2 export, then contrasts file handoff with
remote synchronisation through [[set-remote]], [[push]], [[clone]], and
[[pull]].
Keeping the same project across all steps avoids incidental variation
in names and lets the transport choice stand out.
The remote half is itself a sequence with one object of learning per
step---name the destination, send, receive elsewhere, stay
current---so the learner experiences the whole round trip rather than
being told it exists.

\subsection{Two variants, one contract}

As in \cref{tut:getting-started}, the tutorial tangles into a novice
entry, [[export-and-share]], and an expert entry,
[[export-and-share-expert]], sharing every step's checks through
common chunks.
The mechanism the expert variant makes visible is the Git machinery
under the transports: what a bundle is, which refspecs a push ships,
and what clone and pull promise about the learner's files.
The novice variant stays with the two workflows---hand over a file,
or sync through a shared place---in everyday vocabulary.

