\subsection{Export a portable learnlog bundle}

The file-handoff half opens with its simplest representative: one
command, one file, ready for playback elsewhere.
The step's object of learning is that the whole log fits in a single
portable file.
The expert extra names the format---a Git bundle---and the atomicity
guarantee that a half-written bundle never exists under the target
name.

<<shared step: export a portable learnlog bundle>>=
# Export a portable learnlog bundle

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+export\b[^\n]*(?<!\S)(?:-o|--output)\b\s+\S+\.learnlog\b'
hint: Pick an output name ending in `.learnlog`.
```

Create a portable bundle file with `learnlog export -o course.learnlog`.
This is the easiest format to hand to someone for playback.
<<novice step: export a portable learnlog bundle>>=
<<shared step: export a portable learnlog bundle>>
<<expert step: export a portable learnlog bundle>>=
<<shared step: export a portable learnlog bundle>>

The file is a Git bundle: the log's whole history in one file.
It is written to a staging file and atomically renamed into place, so
a half-written bundle never exists under the name you asked for.
@

\subsection{Export a ProgSnap2 dataset}

The same project, the same [[export]] command, a different flag: the
minimal pair against the bundle step makes the purpose of the format
the only thing that varies.
The step's object of learning is that the export format follows from
what the recipient will do---playback wants a bundle, analysis wants
ProgSnap2.
The expert extra places the format in its research context and points
forward to the analysis tutorial that consumes the file.

<<shared step: export a progsnap2 dataset>>=
# Export a ProgSnap2 dataset

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+export\b[^\n]*--progsnap2\b'
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+export\b[^\n]*(?<!\S)(?:-o|--output)\b\s+\S+\.progsnap2\b'
hint: Use `--progsnap2` when the goal is analysis rather than playback.
```

Export the same log as a ProgSnap2 zip, for example with
`learnlog export --progsnap2 -o course.progsnap2`, so it can be used
for CS education analysis workflows.
<<novice step: export a progsnap2 dataset>>=
<<shared step: export a progsnap2 dataset>>
<<expert step: export a progsnap2 dataset>>=
<<shared step: export a progsnap2 dataset>>

ProgSnap2 is a standard dataset format in computing-education
research, meant for analysis tools rather than learnlog playback; the
`analysing-progsnap2` tutorial picks up exactly this file.
@

\subsection{Configure a remote repository}

The remote half begins by naming the destination, before anything is
sent: the learner separates \emph{where} the shared copy lives from
the acts of sending and receiving.
Hint and prose diverge by audience vocabulary---the novice hears
\enquote{a shared place}, the expert \enquote{a bare repository or a
URL}---while the checks stay shared.

<<checks: configure a remote repository>>=
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+set-remote\s+\S+'
<<novice step: configure a remote repository>>=
# Configure a remote repository

```tutorial-step
<<checks: configure a remote repository>>
hint: Point the remote at the shared location, e.g. `../course.git`.
```

Choose where the shared copy of your log should live and configure it
with `learnlog set-remote ../course.git`.
For practising on one machine, a path next to your project works well;
for sharing across machines, use an address from a Git hosting service
instead.
<<expert step: configure a remote repository>>=
# Configure a remote repository

```tutorial-step
<<checks: configure a remote repository>>
hint: Point `origin` at a Git repository URL or local bare repo path.
```

Choose a remote repository and configure it with
`learnlog set-remote ../course.git`.
For local practice, a bare repository path such as `../course.git`
works well; for sharing across machines, pass a real URL instead.
The remote is stored as an ordinary Git remote named `origin` in the
hidden `.learnlog/` repository.
@

\subsection{Push the log to the remote}

The step's object of learning is that one command sends the whole
recorded history to the named destination.
The novice's hint drops the word \emph{branches}; the expert extra
explains what travels---explicit branch and tag refspecs---so the
milestone tags from \cref{tut:playback-and-tagging} arrive too, where
a plain Git push would leave lightweight tags behind.

<<checks: push the log to the remote>>=
required_patterns:
  - learnlog push
<<novice step: push the log to the remote>>=
# Push the log to the remote

```tutorial-step
<<checks: push the log to the remote>>
hint: Run `learnlog push` after `set-remote` to send your log.
```

Run `learnlog push` to send the whole recorded history to the shared
location you configured.
<<expert step: push the log to the remote>>=
# Push the log to the remote

```tutorial-step
<<checks: push the log to the remote>>
hint: Push after `set-remote` so the remote receives all branches.
```

Run `learnlog push` to send the whole recorded history to `origin`.
The push names explicit branch and tag refspecs, so lightweight
milestone tags---like the one from the playback tutorial---travel with
the history instead of being left behind.
@

\subsection{Clone the log somewhere else}

Receiving completes the round trip: the learner plays the other
machine.
The step's object of learning is that a clone reproduces the shared
log in a fresh place.
The expert extra states the safety property: the copy is staged and
only moved into place once complete, and the clone configures itself,
so an interrupted clone never leaves a half-project behind.

<<shared step: clone the log somewhere else>>=
# Clone the log somewhere else

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: 'learnlog\s+clone\s+\S+'
hint: Try cloning into a second directory to simulate another machine.
```

In another empty directory, use `learnlog clone <remote>` to retrieve
the shared `.learnlog/` repository.
<<novice step: clone the log somewhere else>>=
<<shared step: clone the log somewhere else>>
<<expert step: clone the log somewhere else>>=
<<shared step: clone the log somewhere else>>

The copy is staged in a temporary directory and only moved into place
once complete, and the clone configures itself on first use, so an
interrupted clone never leaves a half-project behind.
@

\subsection{Pull later updates}

The closing step turns the one-shot copy into an ongoing
synchronisation: its object of learning is that a clone can stay
current.
The expert extra gives the guarantee that makes pulling safe to
recommend: the merge happens inside the object database, never touches
the files the learner is working on, and aborts cleanly on conflict.

<<shared step: pull later updates>>=
# Pull later updates

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - learnlog pull
hint: After new commits are pushed, run `learnlog pull` in the clone.
```

When the original project has received more recorded runs and pushed
them, update the clone with `learnlog pull`.
<<novice step: pull later updates>>=
<<shared step: pull later updates>>
<<expert step: pull later updates>>=
<<shared step: pull later updates>>

The pull fetches and merges inside the object database: it never
touches the files you are working on, and if the histories conflict it
aborts cleanly and leaves the clone as it was.
@
